# Global rules

## Response style

- Keep answers short and in plain English.
- Lead with the answer. Details after, only if needed.
- No jargon unless needed. No long summaries at the end.
- Use tables or bullets when they make things clearer.

## Ask before deleting

- Never delete files or folders without asking first. Name what will be deleted and wait for a yes.
- Includes `rm`, `find -delete`, `git clean`, `git rm`, `git reset --hard`, `git restore`,
  and `git checkout -- <file>`.
- Normal file edits do not need asking.

## Git identity

| Folder | Commit email |
|---|---|
| `~/personal/` | `you@example.com` |
| `~/work/client-a/` | `you@client-a.example` |

- `~/.gitconfig` sets this by folder (`includeIf "gitdir:..."`). Do not override `user.*`,
  use `--author`, or set `GIT_AUTHOR_*` env vars.
- Never commit unless the user asks. Always confirm before any push.
- `~/work/`: never commit, push, pull, merge, rebase, reset, or tag. The user does this.
  Suggest the commit message instead.

## Commit messages

```
<type>(<optional scope>): <what changed, plain English, lowercase, no full stop>

<optional body: why, not how. Wrap at 72 chars.>
```

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`, `test`.
- Subject at most 72 characters, written as an instruction ("add", not "added").

## Public vs private repos

- Check which kind of repo you are writing into before you write. Unknown = public.
- Public repos: generic content only. No client names, hostnames, real IPs, live output,
  kubeconfigs, or inventories. Use sample IPs like `192.0.2.x` or `203.0.113.x`.
- No credentials or tokens in any repo, private included.

## Live cluster lock

Never change any live cluster.

- Do not run: create, apply, edit, patch, replace, delete, scale, label, annotate, rollout,
  drain, cordon, taint, exec, rsh, debug, cp, port-forward, or any `oc adm` command.
- No Argo CD syncs, `helm` install/upgrade/uninstall/rollback, `terraform` apply/destroy,
  or `ansible-playbook`. Do not work around this (scripts, `bash -c`, curl to the API).
- Read-only is fine: `get`, `describe`, `logs`, `events`, `top`, `explain`.
- If a change is needed: explain the impact and give the exact command. The user runs it.
